hello everybody we are back for another  vlog I'm enjoying doing these vlogs for  you guys I hope you guys like them too  if you do let me know below if you hate  them um let me know that too I guess  um but anyways I know I look more  glamorous and I normally do but that's  because I had the hair and makeup gods  work on me today for a TV segment and  yeah it always turns out way better than  I could ever pull off myself  but anyways on that note we're gonna be  talking about the holidays which are  coming up oh my gosh I need to start  Christmas shopping that's for sure I am  in no way ready for this but I think the  holidays can be both a joyous time and  also really stressful time for some  people depending on people's  relationship with their families with  money goals and also their relationship  with food and their bodies now I want to  acknowledge that I think all family  relationships are unique and complicated  in their own way but I think that even  in the most loving tight-knit families  commentary around food and body can be  so common and it often does not feel  very supportive and I think that this  time of year the holidays can be  particularly triggering for a lot of  people for a few reasons  so one I think that a lot of the  struggles that a lot of us face when it  comes to body image stemmed from our  childhood or when we were young and and  some of the messages that we were given  when we were young so seeing certain  family members can often kind of hash up  some of those memories too the holidays  are a time when there's just a lot of  food around so food becomes the topic of  a lot of small talk when we're kind of  tired of talking about the weather and  three you know a lot of us don't  actually see our family members all that  often so any changes to our body shape  or size can often seem more dramatic if  a family member is remembering us at a  completely different life stage now I  guess I'm kind of lucky that in my  family where I was growing up nobody  really ever talked about diets weight  loss or come  what people were eating that was just  not something that was really discussed  at dinner time um but I've since been at  the dinner table with a lot of other  unnamed people where this kind of talk  is completely the norm and I since  learned that as a woman living and  breathing in diet culture getting  unsolicited advice about your diet or  your body is pretty freakin common and I  get like a double dose of scrutiny as a  registered dietician about anything that  I eat or anything to do with my body and  I literally cannot go to a dinner party  without somebody coming up to me and  telling me about their diet or comparing  what's on their plate what's on my plate  so that's fine we probably all actually  observed food and body shaming at  different times in our lives and you  know depending on different social  situations you've probably played the  role of both the victim and the  perpetrator  so like for example maybe you're at your  holiday feast your family your family  event and you're kind of feeling full  and your grandmother's offering you pie  and you reject and she screams at you  what's wrong with you you're so skinny  you know are you gonna dye it or maybe  you're out like a buffet and you're  having a little slice of cheese and  you're putting those little little  pastries on your on your plate and your  mom comes up to you and she motions at  your hips and gives you the look and  she's like oh you you're really sure you  need that extra slice of cheese or like  maybe you're up for like a festive meal  with your girlfriends and you're just  like craving a nice crunchy fresh salad  and all your girlfriends start to tease  you and and and call you a diet debbie  downer and basically pressure you into  ordering the burger and fries like them  even when a commentary is not directed  at you it can still be hurtful and  impactful I know like a lot of families  watch holiday shows and holiday movies  together and it seems like a freaking  Christmas miracle if somebody doesn't  comment on some kind of celebrity and  the fact that  oh she's gotten grossly skinny or she's  she's gave a few pounds since her last  appearance there are probably so many  examples of this especially at the  holidays so if you guys have some  examples or experiences that  want to share I'd love to hear about  them in the comments below but what is  interesting to me is that even though  other people's food choices and bodies  don't really have like a whole lot of  impact on us we still feel the need to  comment on them when those choices don't  coincide with our own and that gets  really confusing when our choices are  not always the same and stagnant you  know throughout the year or every single  time we see someone I mean I cannot tell  you how many times I have been at a  family dinner and I basically begged to  have dessert and then the very next day  the exact same person will like look at  me disgust if I choose myself to order  dessert on my own so why do we feel that  we need to engage in this level of  judgment especially around the holidays  well I think there's definitely a few  reasons one there's the jealousy factor  sometimes I think people are just  jealous and and kind of longing for a  more balanced relationship with food you  know they see someone at the dinner  table who's I to have a modest Lysa pie  and a big salad at dinner time and not  need to go into that all-or-nothing  cheat game mode and then start  restricting again tomorrow  I mean body Envy is a whole other topic  and I'm sure that also can play into it  as well but I've seen a lot of body  shaming happening too and by a whole lot  of people with different body shapes and  sizes more importantly I think a lot of  people are trying to kind of justify  their own unhealthy relationship with  food so it's kind of like the idea that  hey if I'm on a restrictive diet and I'm  miserable then you should also be on a  restrictive diet and miserable or if I'm  having a cheat day and gonna eat all the  things and you should also have your  cheat day and eat all the things so it's  kind of like that projecting that we  were talking about in my haze versus  weight loss video in that a person who's  restricting may project their militant  view on food to everyone else around  them the other possibility and this one  is often quoted to justify the hurtful  commentary when a food chamber is called  out is that the family member is just  genuinely concerned about your health  and I think that's probably really true  often I don't think  family members make comments like this  to be mean or malicious or hurtful but I  also know that a research suggests that  people who are fat shamed tend to engage  less not more in health promoting  behaviors and be the holiday table is  just not the place  there are kinder ways to help support  somebody's health journey embarrassing  them at the family dinner table is not  one of them so that being said I thought  it'd be really important to arm you with  some shock responses and strategies that  some my dietician colleagues and I have  come up with and I think they're really  fun so if someone were to say to me Oh  Abbi I could never have both wine and  dessert I would reply oh I've just  enjoying my holidays and I'm glad you  found something that works for you but I  have found that listening to my body and  finding pleasure in food has been really  successful for me both for my physical  and emotional health if someone says to  me oh my gosh  Abbi why aren't you having pie you're so  skinny I would kill to have some pie I  would say back oh you know it looks so  delicious but honestly I've worked  really hard at trying to tune into my  body's needs and I think I would enjoy  this so much more when I've got a little  bit more room in my belly tomorrow so  can I take all the slice something like  that now if somebody makes a comment  about my body changing either getting  bigger or smaller or whatever I probably  say something like oh I didn't realize  you were looking at my body so closely  hmm I think that will shut them up  really fast or if you're feeling like  really sassy because hey sometimes what  happens and some people just need to be  told off you can also say something like  wow I mean please do not project your  insecurities on me I am really  comfortable with my body and food and I  really hope that you two can find that  sense of peace now regardless of which  end of the conversation you end up on  it's important to remember that our  bodies and food are really  really personal so for the days that  you're a victim of food shaming or body  shaming remember that the choices that  you make around food are yours and yours  alone and it's never your responsibility  to make someone happy based on what you  choose to eat or not eat if somebody  cannot find happiness in their own food  choices it's a pretty good indicator and  good set of evidence that they need to  work on their own relationship with food  and maybe the kind thing to do would be  to reach out if you feel up to it and  offer some support to help them get  there meanwhile for the days that you  are the perpetrator and listen I get  that dye culture is so pervasive that  we're all guilty of this sometimes but I  promise you that the flavor of your food  will not change based on what other  people are eating or weighing your kale  will not taste better if everyone else  is eating it and your cake will not  taste stale if you're consuming it alone  so if we all work at this together I  believe we can all settle into happier  healthier places with our bodies  ourselves and food and actually enjoy  the holiday season so on that note I  wish you a big big big happy holidays  from my kitchen here in Abby's kitchen  to yours if you like this video please  give it the thumbs up leave me a comment  below if you have any experiences around  food and body shaming at the holidays  and what are some of your responses to  family members who make not so welcome  remarks don't forget to subscribe to the  channel and I will see you next time on  Pappy's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

